#{[17592186119640 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 0,2,0 \\rangle$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $\\mathbf{n}$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{r} - \\mathbf{p}) \\cdot \\mathbf{n} = 0,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $\\mathbf p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨0,2,0⟩ & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119641 "We need a vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t_1,t_2,t_3 \\rangle$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $\\mathbf{r} \\cdot \\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle  = 3$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2, -1, 4 \\rangle.$ So, $\\mathbf{v} \\perp \\mathbf{n}$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-1,4⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2{t}_{1}-{t}_{2}+4{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =2{t}_{1}+4{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}=\\langle t_1, 2t_1 + 4t_3, t_3 \\rangle,\n$$\n\nwhere $t_1,t_3 \\in (-\\infty,\\infty).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $\\mathbf{v}$ equals $0.$ So, we must set $t_1=0.$ \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is nonzero, we can pick any $t_3 \\neq 0.$ For example, setting $t_3=1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 0,\\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{4}, \\, \\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{1} \\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\dfrac{b}{c} = 4.$"] [17592186119643 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 9,-2,3 \\rangle$ and the normal vector of the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 3,-4,7 \\rangle.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =⟨9,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,7⟩ & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z & =56 \\\\ 3x-4y+7z-56 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119644 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -1,4,8 \\rangle.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{n}=\\langle -5,3,2 \\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =⟨-1,4,8⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-5,3,2⟩ & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z & =33 \\\\ -5x+3y+2z-33 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119645 "First, from the coefficients in equation of the plane, we get that the normal vector of $\\Pi$ is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 2,-1,1 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $\\Pi$ for $z\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-y+z+13 & =0 \\\\ z & =-2x+y-13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0,$ we get $z=-13.$ So, the point $P(0,0,-13)$ lies on $\\Pi.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle 0,0,-13 \\rangle.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-13⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,-1,1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119646 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,-6 \\rangle.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v_1}$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-16i-4j-14k \\\\  & =⟨-16,-4,-14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =⟨-3,2,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $\\mathbf{r}=\\langle x,y,z \\rangle$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-16,-4,-14⟩ & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z & =124 \\\\ -16x-4y-14z-124 & =0 \\\\ 8x+2y+7z+62 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119647 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}=\\mathbf{p}+ s\\mathbf{b}+ t \\mathbf{c},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{p}$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $\\mathbf{b}$ and $\\mathbf{c}$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $\\mathbf{p}=\\langle -3,2,5 \\rangle,$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are $\\mathbf{b}= \\langle 17,3,15 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{c}= \\langle 5,0,-8 \\rangle.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨-3,2,5⟩+s\\,⟨17,3,15⟩+t\\,⟨5,0,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119648 "The direction vectors of the lines are $\\mathbf{v_1}=\\langle  -1,7,2  \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{v_2}=\\langle 2\\sqrt{3},9,-8 \\rangle$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $C(2,50,-19)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $\\mathbf{c}=\\langle 2,50,-19 \\rangle.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =c+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨2,50,-19⟩+s\\,⟨-1,7,2⟩+t\\,⟨2\\sqrt{3},9,-8⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186119649 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\\overrightarrow{AC},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨7,5,4⟩-⟨-1,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,1,7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨2,-2,8⟩-⟨-1,4,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-6,11⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $\\mathbf{a}=\\langle -1,4,-3 \\rangle$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s\\overset{\\to }{AB}+t\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\ r & =⟨-1,4,-3⟩+s⟨8,1,7⟩+t⟨3,-6,11⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** The form of the final equation depends on our choice of vectors and the point on the plane. We could have picked other vectors. Likewise, we could have chosen $B$ or $C$ instead of $A$ as our point on the plane. So, the answer is *not unique*!"] [17592186119664 "From the parametric equation, we have that $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 4, 2, -1 \\rangle$ and $\\mathbf{w} = \\langle 4, -3, 2 \\rangle$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 4 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =i-12j-20k \\\\  & =⟨1,-12,-20⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n}=\\langle 1, -12, -20 \\rangle.$"] [17592186120217 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨2,-5,3⟩,$ and $n=⟨-1,2,-3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =⟨2,-5,3⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,-3⟩ & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120218 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{AB}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{AC},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨6,-8,0⟩-⟨3,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-9,-5⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{AC} & =⟨-2,1,3⟩-⟨3,1,5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-5,0,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $a=⟨3,1,5⟩$ of the point $A,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s\\overset{\\to }{AB}+t\\overset{\\to }{AC} \\\\ r & =⟨3,1,5⟩+s⟨3,-9,-5⟩+t⟨-5,0,-2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120225 "The direction vectors of the lines are ${v}_{1}=⟨3,2,5⟩$ and ${v}_{2}=⟨0,6,2⟩$, respectively. These vectors are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nWe also know that the point $A(5,-3,1)$ lies on the plane and its position vector is $a=⟨5,-3,1⟩.$  \n\nSo, the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =a+s{v}_{1}+t{v}_{2} \\\\ r & =⟨5,-3,1⟩+s⟨3,2,5⟩+t⟨0,6,2⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120237 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨8,3,-1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨4,3,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨4,3,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =⟨8,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨4,3,-1⟩ & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z & =42 \\\\ 4x+3y-z-42 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120247 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-2,2,0⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=3-4t \\\\ y &=1+6t \\\\ z &=-1+2t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨3,1,-1⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-4,6,2⟩}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-4,6,2⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-4,6,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =⟨-2,2,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,6,2⟩ & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120248 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩=-14$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨6,-2,0⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-2,0⟩ & =0 \\\\ 6{t}_{1}-2{t}_{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},3{t}_{1},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the third component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{3}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,3,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186120278 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,-7,5⟩$ and $w=⟨2,12,5⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -7 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 12 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & -7 & 5 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-95i+20j-10k \\\\  & =⟨-95,20,-10⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-95,20,-10⟩.$"] [17592186120283 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨1,0,3⟩.$\n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 5 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3i-15j+15k \\\\  & =⟨3,-15,15⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =⟨1,0,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-15,15⟩ & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z & =48 \\\\ 3x-15y+15z-48 & =0 \\\\ x-5y+5z-16 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120284 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨1,-1,4⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-y+4z+16 & =0 \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}x+\\frac{1}{4}y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=-4.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-4)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-4⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-4⟩)\\cdot ⟨1,-1,4⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186120508 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation of the plane, $r\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩=-12,$  we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,-3,1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-3,1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}-3{t}_{2}+{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =3{t}_{2}-3{t}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},3{t}_{2}-3{t}_{1}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ eqauls $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{1}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{1}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨1,\\,0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{1}{-3}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$"] [17592186193312 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186193319 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"] [17592186198772 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points $P(x,y)$ satisfy the condition\n\n$$\nx\\in \\{-3,-2,-1\\},\\,y\\in \\{-4,-3,-2,-1\\}.\n$$\n\nIn other words,\n\n$$\n-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-4\\le y\\le -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,-3\\le x\\le -1,\\,-4\\le y\\le -1\\}.\n$$"] [17592186198774 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the curve $y={x}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe must also include the curve $y={x}^{2}$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le {x}^{2}\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le {x}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186198777 "The points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=2x-3.$\n\nTherefore, a point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<2x-3\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=2x-3$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\ny\\le 2x-3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y\\le 2x-3}.\n$$"] [17592186198788 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=-x$ and *below* the line $y=2.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>-x,\\,y<2.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n-x<y<2.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the line $y=-x$ since the boundary is included in the set (the curve is solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbol above as follows:\n\n$$\n-x\\le y<2\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,-x\\le y<2\\}.\n$$"] [17592186198790 "The equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the elliptical boundary of our region has horizontal radius $a=3$ and vertical radius $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich we can write as\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=36.\n$$\n\nWe're interested in describing all points that lie *inside* the ellipse. So, let's pick any point inside the region, say, $(0,0).$ Substituting its coordinates into the equation of the boundary, we obtain\n\n$$\n4(0)^{2}+9(0)^{2}\\,<\\,36.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the points $P(x,y)$ in our region are any real numbers that satisfy the condition\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36.\n$$\n\nNotice that we use $<$ since the boundary is excluded from the set (the boundary curve is dashed).\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}<36\\}.\n$$"] [17592186198793 "The points in our set include all those that lie *above* the line $y=3$ and *below* the line $y=2x+3.$\n\nA point $P(x,y)$ lies *inside* the shaded region if the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n$$\ny>3,\\,y<2x+3.\n$$\n\nCombining this into a single condition, we have\n\n$$\n3<y<2x+3.\n$$\n\nWe must also include the lines $y=3$ and $y=2x+3$ since the boundaries are included in the set (the lines are solid). So, we change the corresponding inequality symbols above as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\le y\\le 2x+3\n$$\n\nMoreover, since we're considering *every* real value in this region, $x\\in R,y\\in R,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in R\\times R={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,3\\le y\\le 2x+3\\}.\n$$"] [17592186198805 "Since all points $P(x,y)$ in our set lie at integer values of $x$ and $y,$ we have $x\\in Z,y\\in Z,$ and therefore \n\n$$\n(x,y)\\in Z\\times Z={Z}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe points in our set include all those that lie *below* the line $y=x-1.$ For those points, the following condition is satisfied:\n\n$$\ny<x-1\n$$\n\nSince the boundary is *not* included in the set (the curve is *not* solid),  no further action must be taken, so we have\n\n$$\ny<x-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the set as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x,y)\\in {Z}^{2}\\,:\\,y<x-1}\n$$"] [17592186203719 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2a+5b)\\times c & =(-2a)\\times c+(5b)\\times c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2a)\\times c+(5b)\\times c & =-2(a\\times c)+5(b\\times c)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values for $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{c}$ and $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{c},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(a\\times c)+5(b\\times c) & =-2\\cdot (i)+5\\cdot (-3i+j) \\\\  & =-2i-15i+5j \\\\  & =-17i+5j\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203720 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n2\\mathbf{a} \\times (\\mathbf{a}+3\\mathbf{b})=( 2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (\\mathbf{a}) + (2\\mathbf{a}) \\times (3\\mathbf{b})\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2a)\\times (a)+(2a)\\times (3b) & =2(a\\times a)+(2\\cdot 3)(a\\times b) \\\\  & =2(a\\times a)+6(a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $\\mathbf{a} \\times \\mathbf{a} = \\mathbf{0}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(a\\times a)+6(a\\times b) & =2(0)+6(-b\\times a) \\\\  & =-6(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given value for $\\mathbf{b} \\times \\mathbf{a}$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6(b\\times a) & =-6(i-j-2k) \\\\  & =-6i+6j+12k\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186204191 "First, we recall the following properties of the cross product:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n(7a-2b)\\times b=(7a)\\times b-(2b)\\times b\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n(7a)\\times b-(2b)\\times b=7(a\\times b)-2(b\\times b)\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $b\\times b=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7(a\\times b)-2(b\\times b) & =7(-b\\times a)-2(0) \\\\  & =-7(b\\times a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Substitute the given value for $b\\times a$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7(b\\times a) & =-7⟨2,-1,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-14,7,-42⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186247121 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"] [17592186247148 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But, \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\,-3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram I only"] [17592186247149 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the whole plane ${R}^{2}$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram III is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None.\""] [17592186262809 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=\\sqrt{5}i-j+2k,$ and $n=\\sqrt{5}i-3j+2k$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes through the point $P({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-p)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $p$ is the position vector of $P.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot (\\sqrt{5}i-3j+2k) & =(\\sqrt{5}i-j+2k)\\cdot (\\sqrt{5}i-3j+2k) \\\\ r\\cdot (\\sqrt{5}i-3j+2k) & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262835 "First, recall that:\n\n- The cross product distributes over addition/subtraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(a\\pm b)\\times c & =a\\times c\\pm b\\times c \\\\ c\\times (a\\pm b) & =c\\times a\\pm c\\times b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product is associative with respect to scalar multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda a)\\times b & =\\lambda (a\\times b) \\\\ a\\times (\\lambda b) & =\\lambda (a\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The cross product of parallel vectors equals the zero vector. In particular,\n\n$$\na\\times a=0.\n$$\n\n- The cross product is anticommutative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\times b & =-b\\times a\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's evaluate our expression:\n\n- First, we use the distributive property:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b-c)\\times (9a-2b) &= ((3b)\\times (9a))-((3b)\\times (2b))-(c\\times (9a))+(c\\times (2b))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we factor out the scalars:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3b-c)\\times (9a-2b) &= ((3b)\\times (9a))-((3b)\\times (2b))-(c\\times (9a))+(c\\times (2b)) \\\\ &= (3\\cdot 9)(b\\times a)-(3\\cdot 2)(b\\times b)-9(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b) \\\\ &= 27(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b)-9(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we use anticommutativity, and the fact that $b\\times b=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}27(b\\times a)-6(b\\times b)-9(c\\times a)+2(c\\times b) &= -27(a\\times b)-6(0)+9(a\\times c)+2(c\\times b) \\\\ &= -27(a\\times b)+9(a\\times c)+2(c\\times b)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Finally, we substitute the given values and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-27(a\\times b)+9(a\\times c)+2(c\\times b) &= -27⟨0,0,1⟩+9⟨0,-1,3⟩+2⟨0,2,0⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨0,0,-27⟩+⟨0,-9,27⟩+⟨0,4,0⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨0,-5,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262837 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩=7$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-3,1,-2⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,1,-2⟩ & =0 \\\\ -3{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}-2{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{2} & =3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,2,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=2.$"] [17592186262838 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩=\\sqrt{2}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨3,2,-1⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨3,2,-1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}-{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{3} & =3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{1},\\,{t}_{2},\\,3{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{1},{t}_{2}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the first component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{1}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{2}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{2}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{1}{2}.$"] [17592186262846 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩=\\sqrt{7}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,1,3⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,1,3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & ={t}_{2}+3{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨{t}_{2}+3{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨3,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=3.$"] [17592186262847 "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,2⟩=\\sqrt{11}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,2,2⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+2{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+2{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨2{t}_{2}+2{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨2,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=2.$"] [17592186262853 "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,-2,3⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(4,1,-3)$ and $B(7,-3,1)$, we can find a vector that is parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨7,-3,1⟩-⟨4,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-4,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨3,-4,4⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ & =⟨1,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-4,4⟩ & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262855 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,-2,5⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(-3,0,2)$ and $B(2,1,3)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨2,1,3⟩-⟨-3,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨5,1,1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-p\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩ & =⟨3,-2,5⟩\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,1,1⟩ & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262857 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-1,1,2⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=5-3t \\\\ y &=2+4t \\\\ z &=-3+t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨5,2,-3⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨-3,4,1⟩}_{v}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩,$ so a normal vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨-3,4,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =⟨-1,1,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-3,4,1⟩ & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262858 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-3,2,1⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+6t \\\\ y &=3-5t \\\\ z &=-2+4t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,-2⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨6,-5,4⟩}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨6,-5,4⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨6,-5,4⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-5,4⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-5,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-5,4⟩ & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262859 "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨4,-2,3⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-1+2t \\\\ y &=3-2t \\\\ z &=5+t\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,5⟩+t\\text{ }\\underbrace{⟨2,-2,1⟩}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨2,-2,1⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =⟨4,-2,3⟩\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,-2,1⟩ & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262860 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨5,2,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,-1,2⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =⟨5,2,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,-1,2⟩ & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z & =11 \\\\ 3x-y+2z-11 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262861 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,3,-1⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨3,4,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨3,4,-1⟩ & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z & =7 \\\\ 3x+4y-z-7 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262862 "First, we note that the position vector of the point $P$ is $p=⟨-2,1,-3⟩$ and the normal vector of the plane is $n=⟨-2,7,-1⟩.$\n\nUsing the dot product we can find an equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =⟨-2,1,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =14.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,7,-1⟩ & =14 \\\\ -2x+7y-z & =14 \\\\ -2x+7y-z-14 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262887 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨3,0,-2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨-2,0,7⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨-2,0,7⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =⟨3,0,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,0,7⟩ & =-20 \\\\ -2x+7z & =-20 \\\\ -2x+7z+20 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262888 "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-5,2⟩.$ From the parametrical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨1,1,-1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨1,1,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-5,2⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above. Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,-1⟩ & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z & =-6 \\\\ x+y-z+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262889 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨4,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4y-2z & =0 \\\\ 2z & =4x+4y \\\\ z & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262890 "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,5,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-z+17 & =0 \\\\ z & =5y+17.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=17.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,17)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,17⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,17⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,5,-1⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262901 "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,3⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-1,4⟩,$ and $q=⟨-1,0,1⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-1,4⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,1,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,1⟩-⟨1,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,2,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-4i+2j+6k \\\\  & =⟨-4,2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =⟨3,-1,4⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,2,6⟩ & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z & =10 \\\\ -4x+2y+6z-10 & =0 \\\\ 4x-2y-6z+10 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262902 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-3,5⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=⟨2,4,1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,3,-1⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2,-3,5⟩+s⟨2,4,1⟩+t⟨1,3,-1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186262903 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $P$ is $p=⟨2,-3,4⟩$ and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=⟨1,5,-1⟩$  and ${v}_{2}=⟨1,2.5,1⟩.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=⟨2,-3,4⟩+s⟨1,5,-1⟩+t⟨1,2.5,1⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186262904 "A parametric equation of a plane is given by \n\n$$\nr=p+sb+tc,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is the position vector of a point on the plane and the vectors $b$ and $c$ are parallel to the plane but not to each other.\n\nHere, the position vector of the given point $S$ is $p=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}$, and the two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other) are ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$  and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSo, a parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\nr=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}+s\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+t\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$"] [17592186262915 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-3,4,1⟩$ and $w=⟨5,1,-2⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 5 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-9i-j-23k \\\\  & =⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-9,-1,-23⟩.$"] [17592186262916 "From the parametric equation, we have that $v=⟨-2,8,0⟩$ and $w=⟨3,-1,-1⟩$ are parallel to the plane.\n\nNow, we need to find a normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of $v$ and $w$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =v\\times w \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\times \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -2 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-8i-2j-22k \\\\  & =⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-8,-2,-22⟩.$"] [17592186262927 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). Let's use the displacement vectors $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{UW},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨4,4,-4⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,6,-7⟩ \\\\ \\overset{\\to }{UW} & =⟨3,7,6⟩-⟨7,-2,3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,9,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨7,-2,3⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+s\\overset{\\to }{UV}+t\\overset{\\to }{UW} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-2,3⟩+s⟨-3,6,-7⟩+t⟨-4,9,3⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262928 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨-4,2,6⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{UV}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{UV} & =⟨2,-1,-3⟩-⟨5,3,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,-4,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $u=⟨5,3,1⟩$ of the point $U,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =u+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{UV} \\\\ r & =⟨5,3,1⟩+s⟨-4,2,6⟩+t⟨-3,-4,-4⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186262929 "First, we need to find two vectors that are parallel to the plane (but not to each other). We already have one vector $v=⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩.$ Let's also use the displacement vector $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨5,1,-1⟩-⟨7,-3,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,4,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we consider the position vector $p=⟨7,-3,4⟩$ of the point $P,$ then the corresponding parametric equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =p+sv+t\\overset{\\to }{PQ} \\\\ r & =⟨7,-3,4⟩+s⟨\\sqrt{5},-3,2⟩+t⟨-2,4,-5⟩,\\,s,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186275420 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a set that does not contain the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace.  In this case, we have that the vector $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ lies inside the given subset. But \n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"."] [17592186275441 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram I is not a subspace of ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram II only"] [17592186275442 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagram III only"] [17592186275443 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram II represents a line that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin, so the line in Diagram II is not a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- Diagram III represents the origin $0=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,,\\,$ which is considered a special (trivial) subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is diagrams I and III only"]}
